Subject: Interaction - 3 different results depending on coding
Message-ID: <fc.0075824e06a445b90075824e06a445b9.6a446fd@fcmail.hum.au.dk>

Hello,

I am interested in an interaction between two factors: desc (position in the sentence, from 1 to 6) and mvc (semantic compatibility, yes or no). The dependent variable is RT and I expected to find an interaction at the 4th word, but depending on how I write the lmer() code, I get 3 different results:

#1
lmer(logRT~trial+LogLengthT+LogLengthNT+desc*mvc+(1|subj)+(1|itemN), raw[which(raw$rt>1 & raw$error ==0),],REML=F)->pk.lmer15

Here R tells me that there is a significant effect at the 6th word (t = 2.74). 
But the factors "desc" and "mvc" in themselves are non-significant, so I then tried to get only the interaction between them using this model:

#2
lmer(logRT~trial+LogLengthT+LogLengthNT+I((desc=='word1')*(mvc=='yes'))+I((desc=='word2')*(mvc=='yes'))+I((desc=='word3')*(mvc=='yes'))+I((desc=='word4')*(mvc=='yes'))+I((desc=='word5')*(mvc=='yes'))+I((desc=='word6')*(mvc=='yes'))+(1|subj)+(1|itemN), raw[which(raw$rt>1 & raw$error ==0),],REML=F)->pk.lmer16

Now the effect is at the 4th word (t = 1.95), and there is suddenly no effect at the 6th word (t = 0.91).
Then I tried to use the other level in the mvc-factor (two levels: yes and no):

#3
lmer(logRT~trial+LogLengthT+LogLengthNT+I((desc=='word1')*(mvc=='no'))+I((desc=='word2')*(mvc=='no'))+I((desc=='word3')*(mvc=='no'))+I((desc=='word4')*(mvc=='no'))+I((desc=='word5')*(mvc=='no'))+I((desc=='word6')*(mvc=='no'))+(1|subj)+(1|itemN), raw[which(raw$rt>1 & raw$error ==0),],REML=F)->pk.lmer17

Now the effect at the 4th word has increased to t = 2.71, and the effect at the 6th word is still insignificant (t = 1.40). 

I am at a loss here, since it seems strange to me that the effect moves as a result of leaving out the non-significant "desc" and "mvc" factors and keeping just the significant interaction. I am also puzzled by the fact that the  t-value changes as a result of using the "no" level in mvc instead of the "yes" level. Should it really matter?

I hope someone can enlighten me.

Thanks in advance.

-Johannes
