>>
>> Scott Jackson <scottuba-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org> writes:
>> Hi Johannes,
>>
>> First a more general comment: I think you need to try to clarify what
>> you mean by "interaction at the nth" word.  So you can estimate an
>> effect of mvc, which means "how much longer are RTs in the "yes" vs.
>> "no" conditions?".  And you can look at that effect at each word
>> position, or you can throw in word position as an interacting factor
>> (as in your first model) and look to see if that interaction (in
>> general) adds significantly to the model.  But the question "does mvc
>> interact with word position at the 4th word" is not entirely
>> well-formed.  You have to say "does mvc have an effect at the 4th
>> word, and does this effect differ from X?" but you have to decide what
>> comparison you want (what X is).  I assume you let R do its default
>> treatment contrasts in the first model.  If that's so, and I'm
>> following your description (the lmer print() output would be helpful),
>> then your results are saying that the effect at the 6th word is
>> different from the effect at the 1st word, and all the interaction
>> terms will be comparing to the 1st word (again, because of R's
>> defaults). So maybe the effect of mvc at the 4th word is not
>> statistically different from the effect of mvc at the 1st word, but
>> the effect at the 6th word is.
>>
>> But because you are coming at the data with an interest in the 4th
>> word, your life might be simplified if you start by re-leveling your
>> factor (assuming the 4th word level is just called "4"):
>>
>> raw$desc.relevel <- relevel(raw$desc, "4")
>>
>> This will make a new factor coded the same as your "desc" factor, but
>> with the levels ordered differently such that the 4th level is your
>> "reference" or "baseline" level, if you leave the treatment contrast
>> coding alone.  So when you run the model with this new desc.relevel
>> factor, your interactions will have the interpretation of "how
>> different is the effect of mvc at position X, compared to the 4th
>> position?" (so you should see "mvcyes:desc.relevel1", etc. but not
>> "mvcyes:relevel4" in the output, I think).  One way to word your
>> hypothesis might be that the "base" effect of mvc should be
>> significant (since "base" here means "at the 4th position"), and the
>> effects of mvc at all other positions should be less/more (not sure
>> about the directions of your predictions here), which would show up as
>> interactions with all the other word position levels.  This would be
>> like re-phrasing your hypothesis that there should be an "interaction
>> at the 4th position" to be "all other positions should show different
>> effects than that observed at the 4th position".
>>
>> But maybe this isn't the best way to re-frame your hypothesis; it's
>> certainly not the only option. In fact, your re-codings in the next
>> two models are another alternative.
>>
>> So what you've done in the second two models is to more explicitly
>> dummy-code your interactions. You've basically created a series of new
>> predictors that stand for "+mvc, 1st word", "+mvc, 2nd word" etc.  The
>> reason it looks like you're getting different results is that now
>> you're not actually testing interactions, you're testing the effect of
>> mvc at each position.  You're creating a dummy variable for "effect of
>> mvc at 1st position", "effect of mvc at 2nd position", etc. Based on
>> how you're describing the results, it sounds like the 4th position may
>> be the only position where the effect of mvc is significant, when you
>> look at the effect word-by-word.
>>
>> However, this doesn't mean that the effect at the 4th position is
>> statistically different from the effect at other positions (i.e., an
>> interaction). In order to test that explicitly, the releveling scheme
>> above is one approach.
>>
>> I'm not 100% sure why you are getting different results between your
>> 2nd and 3rd models.  If there are any NAs in your data, that might
>> throw things off, since == can work in unexpected ways with NAs. But
>> maybe there's something else funny going on.
>>
>> Finally, because you are doing a linear lmer (as opposed to glmer),
>> you can get a straight-up ANOVA table by running:
>>
>> anova(pk.lmer15)
>>
>> This will give you F-values (and numerator dfs, but not denominator
>> dfs, so no exact p-values) for each predictor (including the
>> interaction), and will basically tell you what a regular ANOVA would
>> tell you: that there is a general interaction (or not) between
>> position and mvc, meaning that the effect of mvc varies significantly
>> somewhere across the different word levels, but it doesn't tell you
>> where.
>>
>> In summary, I think the strongest test for your hypothesis would be to
>> run the re-leveled analysis that I describe first.  This would tell
>> you (a) whether the effect of mvc at the 4th word is significant, and
>> (b) whether this effect is significantly different at other word
>> positions, compared to the effect at the 4th word.  It sounds like you
>> may get somewhat equivocal results, though, which are suggestive that
>> the 4th position is where the action is, but not sufficient to rule
>> out that the other positions are really different.  How you want to
>> deal with that is, of course, up to you :-)
>>
>> hope this helps,
>> -scott
>>
