Message-ID: <1831B520E6E23C41ACD7FDCB342E84E210E5C761@staff-mail1.qmu.ac.uk>

Hi Zhenguang,
 
As long as your factors have only two levels then when you center them the results you get will correspond to main effects in ANOVAs.
 
When both fixed effects are centered the intercept isn't a particular combination of factors (e.g. A1:B1) as it would when you use treatment coding (1s and 0s). Instead, the intercept represents the grand mean and the result for each predictor is the effect independent of other predictors (as you get in an ANOVA).
 
HTH,
 
Ian

