Hi Zhenguang,

Seems like you're done =). The first of the outputs you attached is the
ANOVA-like variant that you asked for.

Formula: Response ~ A * B + (1 | Subject) + (1 | Item)
Fixed effects:
            Estimate Std. Error z value Pr(>|z|)
(Intercept)   0.8186     0.3815   2.146   0.0319 *
A1           -0.2569     0.1078  -2.384   0.0171 *
B1           -1.4164     0.1217 -11.639  < 2e-16 ***
A1:B1        -0.6646     0.1098  -6.054 1.41e-09 ***

As for the interpretation:

1) there is a significant main effect of factor A. in order to understand
it, I'd need to see what the levels of A are. You can do contrasts(dat$A)
to see how levels were assigned to -1 and 1 (the default values for
contr.sum(2) in R). So, for a perfectly balanced data set, the absolute
value of the coefficient of A1 gives the distance of each level of A from
the overall mean. The coefficient also tells yo whether the level assigned
value 1 results in a larger outcome (positive coefficient) or a smaller
outcome (negative coefficient) than the level assigned value -1 by
contr.sum(2). Finally, the distance between the means of the two levels of
A is two times the coefficient of A1.

2) there is a significant main effect of factor B. etc.

3) there is a significant interaction of A and B, such that (and this is
just one way of putting it) the effect of A is larger (more negative) for
contrast(B) == 1 (i.e., for the level of B which was assigned the value 1
by contr.sum(2)). Similarly, we could say that the effect of B is larger
(more negative) for contrasts(A) == 1.

4) Note that under default contr.sum coding (-1,1) the interaction values
are also -1 or 1. So, the above output tells us a couple of things. For
example, while the numerical effect of B interacts with A, it's always in
the same direction. The same does *not* hold for A. The numerical effect of
A changes direction based on the value of B. Note that we would have to run
additional regressions in order to determine whether the simple effects are
significant, e.g., a reasonable question to ask based on the output above
is whether the reversal effect of A depending on B is significant (i.e. are
the simple effects both significant but in different directions or, e.g.,
is one simple effect significant but the numerically reversed effect is
not).

5) So the predicted cell means (log-odds) are:

contrast(A)==1 & contrast(B)==1: intercept + coef(A1) + coef(B1) +
coef(A1:B1)
contrast(A)==1 & contrast(B)==-1: intercept + coef(A1) - coef(B1) -
coef(A1:B1)
contrast(A)==-1 & contrast(B)==1: intercept - coef(A1) + coef(B1) -
coef(A1:B1)
contrast(A)==-1 & contrast(B)==-1: intercept - coef(A1) - coef(B1) +
coef(A1:B1)

So, for example, the predicted log-odds for contrast(A)==1 & contrast(B)==1
are -1.5669 and the predicted probability of a positive outcome, p
(response == p), is plogis(-1.5669) =  0.1726588. Similarly, the predicted
probability for contrast(A)== -1 & contrast(B)== -1 is 0.8614398.

I hope I didn't switch things around (I didn't run any code) and that this
helps.

Florian


