>>>>         Hi Zhenguang,
>>>>         As long as your factors have only two levels then when you
>>>>         center them the results you get will correspond to main
>>>>         effects in ANOVAs.
>>>>         When both fixed effects are centered the intercept isn't a
>>>>         particular combination of factors (e.g. A1:B1) as it would
>>>>         when you use treatment coding (1s and 0s). Instead, the
>>>>         intercept represents the grand mean and the result for each
>>>>         predictor is the effect /independent/ of other predictors
>>>>         (as you get in an ANOVA).
