some notes ...
let's start with Sarkars book

http://lmdvr.r-forge.r-project.org/figures/figures.html

here Ch. 11 you can find the way to explore the trellis object

library(lattice)
show.settings() # graphical representation for the trellis object features
tp <- trellis.par.get() # now let's explore the trellis object
class(tp) # it's a list
names.tp <- lapply(tp, names) # so let's extract the list elements
names(names.tp)[1:18] # take the first 18
str(trellis.par.get('superpose.symbol')) # take a look inside: what  
are the arguments

as you can this is a nested named list. show.settings() relates the  
list-entry-names to the graphic elements (strips, boxes - you name it).
You can change all this locally, e.g., for the next plot, with  
xypllot(...,par.settings='your settings') or globally with  
trellis.par.set('your settings').
The feeling of R-Graphics beeing arcane may be changed by reading R  
Graphics by Paul Murrel (it comes with a nice explaination about  
'grid' on which lattice and ggplot2 is stemed).
the best,
René



