Thanks to all. I eventually stumbled across the magic words that I needed to 
make lattice do what I want. I hereby describe them for deposit into the r-lang 
archive, in case my future self tries to google this again.

Remember that I'm using lattice specifically to plot interactions between 
continuous variables. With that in mind, here are my answers to myself:

(1) The label-boxes at the top of each panel are called "strips" (as Alex Fine 
pointed out), and the shaded part that shows what subset is represented in each 
panel is called the "shingle". Actually, strictly speaking, as "?shingle" 
explains: "A shingle is a data structure used in Trellis, and is a 
generalization of factors to ‘continuous’ variables. It consists of a numeric 
vector along with some possibly overlapping intervals." No wonder I couldn't 
find the right term.

(2) Once I re-stumbled across this term, I searched my own computer and 
discovered that as I had half remembered, I had already had some code from an 
earlier project for changing the color of the shingle, the origins of which are 
lost in the mists of time. Guess which of the following is the correct way to do 
this:

(a)
shingle.color <- "red"

(b)
x <- trellis.par.get("strip.shingle")
x[["col"]][1] <- "red"
trellis.par.set("strip.shingle", x)

If you answered (b), you are correct.

But my stupidity is such that in my new code I had already turned off all color 
via the following:

trellis.device(color = FALSE)

The reason the shingle was still coming out colored was that I didn't notice 
that I followed the above with the lines in (b), which I had just copied and 
pasted in from the earlier project. But the happy ending is that I was now able 
to make the shingle "darkgrey", since the default B&W version makes the shingle 
too light for my taste.

(3) How you put your own text into those label boxes, instead of having the 
variable name appear by default, is related to what Alex Fine explained to do 
when using categorical factors, except since we're now dealing with continuous 
variables, you instead use this:

xyplot( ...
  strip = strip.custom(var.name = yercoolvarname)
  ...)

Hope this helps.

Sincerely, my past self

