From: ernesto.guerra-VYBfkVvgaNUmRHQWxfeBDQ at public.gmane.org (Ernesto Guerra)
Date: Fri, 18 Jan 2013 15:59:22 +0100
Subject: convergence in LMER and order of factor
Message-ID: <17579_1358521165_ZZi0d5z7x6hvj.00_50F9634A.4090403@uni-bielefeld.de>

Dear all,
We have a general question about LMER convergence. When our full model 
did not converge, we tried changing the order of the factors, while 
retaining all the factors. We found that this model (which differed from 
the initial model only in order in which the factors are entered in the 
model) converged. This is what happened.

The initial model was:
(1) /lmer (first pass ~ A * B + (1 + A * B | participant) + (1 + A * B | 
item) , data/
(factors are centered using: /data$A = scale(as.numeric(data$A)))/.
This did not converge, so we first tried by increasing the number of 
iterations in the estimation procedure by adding to the model: /control 
= list(maxIter = 3000)/
(2) /lmer (first pass ~ A * B + (1 + A * B | participant) + (1 + A * B | 
item) , data, control = list (maxIter = 3000))/ But (2) still did not 
converge.

Then we tried a different model like (3).
(3) /lmer (first pass ~ A * B + (1 + B * A | participant) + (1 + B * A | 
item) , data, control = list (maxIter = 3000))/. And this one did converge.

As you can see (3) has the same number of factors as (1) and (2) but the 
order is different.

QUESTIONS:
1. Why is there convergence in (3) and not in (1) and (2)? We have not 
seen in any references that changing the order of factors should make a 
difference in achieving convergence.
2. Can we rely on the output of model (3)?

Could someone please explain?

All the best,

-- 
