In-Reply-To: <17579_1358521165_ZZi0d5z7x6hvj.00_50F9634A.4090403@uni-bielefeld.de>
References: <17579_1358521165_ZZi0d5z7x6hvj.00_50F9634A.4090403@uni-bielefeld.de>
Message-ID: <9F281EF3-761E-44FC-BECB-D2B7B8A274C8@glasgow.ac.uk>

Hi Ernesto!

As far as my understanding of LMEMs goes, the order of the terms in the random effects part of the formula following the "1+" should not affect whether or not the model converges. The formula is just a way of specifying the structure of the variance-covariance matrix, and A*B corresponds to the same structure as B*A.  Maybe someone with more knowledge about the ins and outs of the estimation procedure could give a reason for why something like this would happen.  It sounds like a bug to me.

In your case I would probably not trust the output from model (3), but would make sure that you are using the most up to date version of the lme4 package, and if not, upgrade and try again.  If it is reproducible in the most recent version, and you have double checked that the data sets and syntax are identical in (2) and (3) apart from the ordering of A and B, then it might be time to file a bug report.

Best,
-Dale

