Message-ID: <07E62C96-2995-4A9F-B8A7-668C16B509C0@ucsd.edu>

Hi Ernesto & co,

As Dale points out, your models 2, and 3 are equivalent to each other and should yield the same results.  Without further information, I would guess that the difference in the results between 2 and 3 is due to issues of numerical precision -- although the models are mathematically equivalent, the accumulated rounding errors in estimating model parameters lead the two function calls in slightly different directions in parameter space, and this gives you the discrepancy.  I wouldn't be surprised if model 2 converged if you increased maxIter even further.  However, as Dale points out, I would be careful about interpreting these model results (probably the likelihood surface is very flat).

I would also suggest posting this question to R-sig-ME.

Best & hope this helps,

Roger

