Dear Zhenguang,

both are legitimate ways. The differences will tend to be minimal. Among
language-audience directed papers, Barr et al 2012/13 [?] contains
comparisons between those methods that confirm that these two methods will
tell you the same thing for reasonably sized data sets like those typical
for psycholinguistic work.

One challenge in the model comparison approach is to determine whether you
should remove only the fixed effect or also its random slope (if there is
one, like in the second case you posted). Perhaps that's enough of a reason
to keep your life simple and go with |t| > 2. FWIW, I tend to have a
footnote that states that the other approach yields the same (or an
appendix that actually give the results), just to let reviewers know (why
make them wonder ...?). That's partly based on the observation that as a
reader/reviewer I tend to like reading that (though I don't mind if it's
not there).

HTH,
Florian

