Subject: Re: queries regarding significance of interaction term, simple effects, and false convergence
In-Reply-To: <CAE3_nMSSKZ8LbBN8G7L7MuZf4eh0==pvkqTR=ahdgN4nsKWEaA@mail.gmail.com>
References: <CAE3_nMSSKZ8LbBN8G7L7MuZf4eh0==pvkqTR=ahdgN4nsKWEaA@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <CA+tkQ_MjTirTPATAkJX2q-0ZC9crVpAQmi6mcW+pDBhfkG8fdw@mail.gmail.com>

Hi Iva,

Thanks for the interesting example!  I've started seeing enough cases
like this where it's beginning to make a little sense to me, so I
thought I'd share what I saw when I poked around a little with your
data.

First, I think it can be helpful to try a few more models and see
which random effects cause your effects to "disappear."  This isn't
always clearly interpretable, but I think it can be a good hint at
what's actually going on in your data. If you try a few things, you'll
find that in your exp1 data, it's the interaction (A:B) slope by
subjects that seems to be doing the most "damage" to your effect. This
could be taken as a hint that you have a great deal of variability
between subjects, and that once you stop assuming that all people must
have the same magnitude/direction of an interaction effect (i.e., once
you model the interaction as a random slope by subject), then the
differences look more like random variation and not about your
experimental factors. Again, this is certainly not *necessarily* the
case, but I think it gives you a place to start looking to understand
your data better.

And indeed, this seems to bear out in this data. First, the variance
captured by the random effects for the interaction is quite large,
which should lead you to expect that people are varying a lot.

Second, I think graphing your data can be the most informative thing
you can do.  Try this:

#######################
library(plyr)
library(ggplot2)
exp1 <- read.delim("exp1.dat")

exp1.means <- ddply(exp1, c("A", "B"), summarize, mean = mean(data), N
= length(data))
ggplot(exp1.means, aes(factor(A), mean)) + geom_bar(aes(fill =
factor(B)), stat = "identity", position = "dodge")

exp1.subjmeans <- ddply(exp1, c("subj", "A", "B"), summarize, mean =
mean(data), N = length(data))
ggplot(exp1.subjmeans, aes(factor(A), mean)) + geom_bar(aes(fill =
factor(B)), stat = "identity", position = "dodge") + facet_wrap(~
subj)
######################

The first plot is the grand means, and shows the interaction pattern
you're hoping(?) to see, which shows up when you leave out the A:B
random slope by subjects.  But the second plot is really instructive,
because it shows the means by subject!  First, there are a couple of
people that show something like the overall pattern (esp. s23), but
there's one person who shows a really different pattern (s02), but
probably more to the point, there are a lot of people with essentially
all zeros in one or more (or all!) conditions.  In other words, the
distribution of 1s in your DV is pretty sparse, and you don't have a
ton of data from each person, so you're getting a lot of cells where
particular people are giving you all one response.  This provides a
likely reason for why you're having convergence problems, too.  It can
be hard to estimate the variance of random effects when some people
are responding the exact same way to every single item!

So I think this is ultimately reassuring that the frustrations you're
experiencing with lmer are actually telling you something: that your
effects are highly variable across people, and that you may need more
responses/person or more people, or maybe a change to the task, or
perhaps some other analysis tools, to more adequately test your
hypotheses. In other words, the models are telling you exactly what
they should in this case: if you ignore the repeated measures nature
of your data, it looks like there is an interaction. But the
variability in this interaction from subject to subject looks like a
great deal of random variation, which makes generalizing this pattern
to new sets of subjects unlikely.

As an aside there's an interesting package I stumbled across recently
that also might be helpful in cases like this.  It's called
"influence.ME", and you get it on CRAN the usual way.  The idea is to
provide some tools to identify levels in your random effects (i.e.,
particular subjects or items) that appear to be overly influential in
your data. "Overly influential" boils down to meaning that if you
remove one person (or item, or whatever) from your data and the
regression results are radically different, that person may be a kind
of outlier with respect to a more general pattern.  People have been
dealing with outlier *observations* for some time; dealing with overly
influential *people* or *items* is somewhat less common, but
essentially the same in principle. The ideas seem sound to me, and the
work is published, and even if you don't want to apply it too much in
the way you report your results, it might be useful in helping to
understand what's going on. The point here is that "too much
by-subject variation" could be caused by just one or two really
eccentric individuals' data (i.e., which may be too influential), or
it might indicate more general variability.  Poking around to see
whether there are specific people who may be overly influential may be
informative.

So here's some code:

############
library(lme4)
exp1.full <- lmer(data ~ A*B + (1+A*B|subj) + (1+A*B|item), data =
exp1, family = "binomial")

library(influence.ME)
library(lattice)

influence.exp1.full <- influence(exp1.full, "subj")
plot(influence.exp1.full, which = "cook")
############

The influence function takes a while to run, because it's essentially
evaluating your model removing one person at a time (or one item, if
you specify "item" instead of "subj").  The plot function here calls
on the lattice package to quickly visualize the Cook's distance for
each person. There is clearly one person who is way off on the end,
which would make them a good candidate for removal as an "outlier" in
their overall pattern.  And this person is the one that had a very
different pattern when we graphed by-subject means: s02.  Clearly
there's something odd about this person compared to the other subjects
in your sample.  Whether that means you should exclude them, or
whether it means you need data from more people to see whether there
are others like them, or whether there's some other variable here that
you're not including that could explain why they're different... well
that would take some more thought. But I thought it was interesting to
see this statistical technique line up so well with what the
by-subject plotting revealed.

Your exp2 data is even more "defective", however, when you look at
things by subject: it looks like you have 17 people who responded "0"
to everything, and 7 who responded "1" to everything!  With such
extreme response patterns, convergence problems are almost a given,
and for good reason.  Try plotting the by-subject means in the same
way, and you'll see what I mean.

Hope this helps!
-scott

