Hi Iva,

phew, I need a grant to answer that one ;). Let me start and see how far I
get.

As I said before on this mailing list, I have some doubts about the
proposal put forward in Barr et al, in particular for models with
non-normal link functions (like yours).

In your particular case, I think the difference between the different
models is due to the fact that your outcome is rather highly skewed towards
0. Specifically, given that you 24 subject and 36 items, many of them will
have 0 observations with outcome 1 in some of the design cells defined by
the 2x2. For example, at least about 2/3s of your items have a mean ML
probability of 0 for the outcome "1" for A=2&B=2. For subjects, that
problem is slightly ameliorated. Adding the random slope to the model will
make it even harder to detect an effect under those circumstances (even if
it's there, which, of course, we don't know). There's a hint in your model
output that the random slope variance estimate might suffer from the
problem I am describing: the (variance) estimates for the random slopes for
the interaction are quite a bit larger than those for the other random
effects. This is particularly striking for the by-item random slope for the
interaction term (notice also the high correlation between that random
slope and the by-item random slopes for lower-order terms in the model -
those could a) tell us something about a cool property of the data, but
they can also result from b) a floor effect, which would be in line with
what I have in mind).

Sorry, that this is not a solution proposal. It's late and I just wanted to
get us started on a response.

With regard to your other question:

On Mon, Jan 21, 2013 at 3:07 PM, Iva Ivanova <iva.m.ivanova-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org>wrote:

> Second, if indeed there is no interaction in my experiment, why is there
> such a big discrepancy between the lmer and ANOVA results?  Does this mean
> that all published (e.g. syntactic priming) experiments that were analyzed
> with ANOVAs should be reevaluated?


Well, on some level that's what I argued in Jaeger (2008), with the caveat
that (lucky us) most results within the .3 < mean proportion < .7 range are
pretty save. Note your data fall *outside *of that range.



> I also brought up the reduced model (exp1.1) for the following reason. I
> understand the need to always use the maximal random effects structure,
> where possible (i.e., when the model converges). However, very frequently
> such models do not converge (although it does in my case), and what is
> reported is the results from a model that converges. But what I am getting
> with my data is quite a difference between a reduced and the full model -
> so how confident can we be in the results of the reduced models - in cases
> when the full models do not converge?
>

That's the question that Barr et al is about. Generally, a case like yours
is pretty clear (prior to considering problems due to the nature of
binomial estimates from finite amounts of data). If model comparison
supports the inclusion of random effect, nobody in their right mind has
proposed that those random effects should be excluded (e.g., Baayen, 2004,
Baayen et al., 2008; Barr, 2008; Dixon, 2008; Jaeger, 2008, etc.). That
fact is easily lost in some discussion of this problem (see Barr et al 2012
for a concise summary of the problem). The case when model comparison
doesn't support the inclusion of a random effect is more complicated.
First, we have to be clear what we mean by that. For example, if any
stepwise procedure is used, it's been known for a long time that this will
increase the Type I error rate (see e.g., Harrell 2001 for a beautifully
concise summary of the problem). However, suppose you compare the largest
RE structure that converges against the smallest RE structure you're
entertaining and you find that the deviance difference is really small (say
less than 3), then the Type I error rate will likely be extremely minimal
(that's the approach we've been using in the past, as also described on the
HLP lab blog; that approach is unfortunately not tested in the Barr et al
2012 paper, at least not in the last version I saw -- I'd be interested to
see the consequences).

Now, in your case, we have the added complexity that your outcome is
binomial and there's a lot of uncertainty (in our field) about what exactly
the consequences of that are for mixed models, ANOVA, etc. (you might have
noticed a few sentences in the Barr et al paper that state that their
simulations are all based on models with a Gaussian link function). So,
that's another open question -- perhaps Barr et al, 2.0 will soon answer it
=). Since Dale has generously shared his library (simgen), the next person
will have a considerably easier time running these simulations. Does
anybody know of simulations of this type (even for non-mixed models)?


