Hi Iva, Florian, Scott and Zhenguang,

Recently, I have found some (I think) unexpected results with logit mixed effect analyses that include random slopes, whereas an intercept-only model shows a more sensible outcome. It seems similar to what Iva gets and similar to Zhenguang's observations in his experiments.

We did an experiment in which participants could produce a referring expression that either contained colour (e.g., the black candle) or an expression that didn't contain it. We have 3 conditions, and the mean proportion colour expressions is:

> tapply(exp1$colouronly, list(exp1$recodedcond), mean)
        1         2         3
0.7903683 0.9226361 0.0093750

Or in log odds:

Recodedcond 1   0.58
Recodedcond 2   1.08
Recodedcond 3   -2.02

If I include random slopes into my model, then the results are:

Generalized linear mixed model fit by the Laplace approximation
Formula: colouronly ~ recodedcond + (recodedcond + 1 | ID) + (recodedcond + 1 | item)
   Data: exp1
   AIC   BIC logLik deviance
 587.6 661.5 -278.8    557.6
Random effects:
 Groups Name         Variance   Std.Dev. Corr
 item   (Intercept)    0.082227  0.28675
        recodedcond2   0.459485  0.67785 -0.667
        recodedcond3 138.211442 11.75634  0.024  0.155
 ID     (Intercept)    0.279673  0.52884
        recodedcond2   0.589284  0.76765 1.000
        recodedcond3  84.347513  9.18409 0.018  0.018 Number of obs: 1022, groups: item, 36; ID, 10

Fixed effects:
             Estimate Std. Error z value Pr(>|z|)
(Intercept)    1.4226     0.2198   6.472 9.64e-11 ***
recodedcond2   1.7341     0.3879   4.470 7.81e-06 ***
recodedcond3 -20.8357     8.7819  -2.373   0.0177 *
---
Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1

Correlation of Fixed Effects:
            (Intr) rcddc2
recodedcnd2  0.259
recodedcnd3 -0.004  0.018

I think this result is unexpected because the significance value for the contrast between conditions 1 and 2 is lower (7.81e-06 ***) than for the condition 1-3 contrast (0.0177 *) even though the log-odds difference between conditions 1 and 2 is much smaller than between 1 and 3. I think the unexpected outcome is probably due to the fact that condition 3 is very close to 0, so the model has problems coming up with a good estimate. (Does that make sense?) Note also that there is a perfect correlation between the intercept and the recodedcond2 contrast, so that may be a reason why it isn't a good idea to include random slopes. (Does that also make sense?)

When I don't include recodedcond as random subject and item slopes, then the results look more sensible:

Generalized linear mixed model fit by the Laplace approximation
Formula: colouronly ~ recodedcond + (1 | ID) + (1 | item)
   Data: exp1
