(Intercept)    1.4613     0.2550   5.730 1.00e-08 ***
recodedcond2   1.2157     0.2455   4.952 7.36e-07 ***
recodedcond3  -6.3344     0.6370  -9.945  < 2e-16 ***
---
Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1

Correlation of Fixed Effects:
            (Intr) rcddc2
recodedcnd2 -0.282
recodedcnd3 -0.129  0.109

My interpretation is that the estimates are better in this case because they are based on more data points per participant/item. It seems to suggest that not including random slopes is sometimes better (perhaps when the mean proportion is close to 0 or 1?)

I have one more practical question: Because the intercept and recodedcond2 are perfectly correlated, could I exclude the recodedcond2 contrast from the random slopes while keeping the recodedcond3 contrast? How would I do this, what would be the formula for removing only the recodedcond2 contrast?

Thanks very much for any responses,

Roger

